    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "This startup&#8217;s self-driving excavators help dig up $600M in new funding",
      "d": "Bedrock Robotics, which is now valued at $4.1 billion, is particularly focused on autonomous excavators, in part because of a growing shortage of skilled operators.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/this-startups-self-driving-excavators-help-dig-up-600m-in-new-funding/",
      "m": "10. 26. PM 05:45",
      "ts": 1793036700000,
      "fullText": "Bedrock Robotics has raised $600 million in a funding round that values the autonomous vehicle technology startup at $4.1 billion, marking a speedy ascent for the two-year-old company. The Series C round, co-led by CapitalG and Emergence Capital, comes two months after the startup reached a key milestone: moving its autonomous excavators beyond testing and pilot programs and putting them to work on active construction sites. Bedrock Robotics has developed a self-driving kit that can be retrofitted onto construction and other worksite vehicles. Excavators outfitted with Bedrock&#8217;s autonomous-driving tech are now commercially operating at a Nevada-based water treatment facility, an earthwork site run by Champion Site Prep in Texas , and a 1.2 million-cubic-yard civil sitework project with Zachry Construction Corporation. Bedrock has raised nearly $1 billion since it was founded in 2024 by a group of autonomous vehicle and robotics industry veterans. Co-founder and CEO Boris Sofman previously led Waymo’s now-shuttered self-driving trucks program. Sofman also co-founded and led Anki Robotics, the maker of the popular Cozmo consumer robot, which shut down in 2019. Other co-founders include Waymo veterans Kevin Peterson, Bedrock&#8217;s chief technology officer, and Ajay Gummalla, who serves as Bedrock&#8217;s vice president of engineering and infrastructure. Tom Eliaz, who previously worked at Segment and Twilio, is also a co-founder and VP of engineering and hardware. The fresh capital will help Bedrock accelerate its transition to commercial work. The company plans to deploy its technology at more construction sites, while expanding the teams and infrastructure needed to support customers in the field. &#8220;We have proven that fully autonomous excavators can perform real work of value alongside the crews and workflows already in place on job sites. The next challenge is delivering that capability to more contractors as a dependable part of how projects get built"
    },
    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Tesla turns to Turo to boost adoption of Full Self-Driving (Supervised)",
      "d": "Eligible Turo hosts will also get a one-month free trial of Tesla's FSD software.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/tesla-turns-to-turo-to-boost-adoption-of-full-self-driving-supervised/",
      "m": "10. 26. PM 12:58",
      "ts": 1793019520000,
      "fullText": "Car rental giant Turo will start advertising which Teslas on its marketplace are outfitted with the automaker&#8217;s advanced driver assistance system, Full Self-Driving (Supervised) — or FSD, a collaboration that could boost further adoption of the driving software. Eligible Turo &#8220;hosts&#8221; who rent their cars on the platform will also receive a free one-month trial of FSD. Turo said it is working with Tesla to directly access the automaker&#8217;s API to verify which cars have FSD enabled. The announcement comes just a few days after Tesla offered updated figures that show the carmaker is finally getting more customers to pay for the software, which can handle most driving tasks but still requires human supervision. Last week, Tesla revealed that around 1.7 million customers have paid for FSD globally, out of more than 10 million cars built and sold to date. The company said more than 75% of customers who took delivery of a car in North America during the third-quarter paid for an FSD subscription. That was a roughly 20% increase from the second quarter, though the company&#8217;s U.S. sales have been in decline . Tesla has spent much of the last year re-working some of its showrooms to focus more on FSD as a selling point. In a post last week, the company&#8217;s chief executive Elon Musk said the effort is working. &#8220;People are buying FSD with a car attached, not the other way around,&#8221; wrote Musk. &#8220;Customers are now coming in and saying: &#8216;Can we get into an FSD drive?&#8217; They&#8217;re not asking for [Model] 3, [Model] Y, or Cybertruck,&#8221; Tesla&#8217;s finance chief Vaibhav Taneja said on last week&#8217;s earnings call. For Turo, the addition of FSD rentals gives the company a chance to boost what are already some of its most heavily rented vehicles. Turo said Tesla&#8217;s Model 3 and Model Y are the third- and fourth-most popular vehicles on its platform this year. &#8220;[T]his integration further increases host earni"
    },
